SUMMARY:THE HISTORY OF CONCRETE
LOCATION:UW Cinematheque\, 4070 Vilas Hall\, 821 University Ave\, Madison\
 , WI 53706
DESCRIPTION:USA | 2026 | DCP| 101 mins.\n\nDirector: John Wilson\n\nUnsure
  of what to do after the conclusion of his essential HBO show How To With
  John Wilson\, first-person filmmaker John Wilson enrolls in a workshop o
 n how to create the perfect Hallmark movie. He then attempts to apply wha
 t he’s learned to a new project: a documentary about concrete. This omn
 ipresent\, overlooked building material leads him down a mind-bending arr
 ay of rabbit holes. With his signature mix of inquisitive wonder\, alert 
 street photography\, and witty\, free-associative editing\, Wilson unerri
 ngly finds funny and roundabout avenues into the profound. (MK)\n\nhttps:
 //cinema.wisc.edu/events/the-history-of-concrete/\n\nSource: UW Cinemathe
 que
URL:https://cinema.wisc.edu/events/the-history-of-concrete/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:cinematheque-2801-0-1790985600@cinema.wisc.edu
DTSTAMP:20261003T000000Z
DTSTART:20261003T000000Z
DTEND:20261003T014600Z
SUMMARY:MIKEY AND NICKY
LOCATION:UW Cinematheque\, 4070 Vilas Hall\, 821 University Ave\, Madison\
 , WI 53706
DESCRIPTION:USA | 1976 | DCP| 106 mins.\n\nDirector: Elaine May\n\nCast: P
 eter Falk\, John Cassavetes\, Ned Beatty\n\nNicky (Cassavetes) is in trou
 ble. Hiding from the mob in a Philadelphia hotel room\, the frantic gangs
 ter calls Mikey (Falk) for help. Little does Nicky know that his old pal 
 is in cahoots with the exasperated hitman (Beatty) hunting him down. As M
 ikey and Nicky roam the city\, director May applies her keen eye for flaw
 ed male characters to this deteriorating friendship\, one that seems dest
 ined to end in a harrowing betrayal. Featuring career-best work from long
 time collaborators Falk and Cassavetes\, this darkly funny\, anxiety-indu
 cing crime story is one of the great American films of the 1970s. (JM)\n\
 nhttps://cinema.wisc.edu/events/mikey-and-nicky/\n\nSource: UW Cinematheq
 ue
URL:https://cinema.wisc.edu/events/mikey-and-nicky/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:cinematheque-2802-0-1791068400@cinema.wisc.edu
DTSTAMP:20261003T230000Z
DTSTART:20261003T230000Z
DTEND:20261004T002200Z
SUMMARY:FOUR NIGHTS OF A DREAMER
LOCATION:UW Cinematheque\, 4070 Vilas Hall\, 821 University Ave\, Madison\
 , WI 53706
DESCRIPTION:France | 1971 | DCP | 82 min. | French with English subtitles\
 n\nDirector: Robert Bresson\n\nCast: Guillaume des Forêts\, Isabelle Wei
 ngarten\, Maurice Monnoyer\n\nSet within a bohemian world in contemporary
  Paris\, Four Nights of a Dreamer follows Jacques\, a lonely artist searc
 hing for the girl of his dreams. One night he saves the beautiful Marthe 
 from plunging into the Seine in despair over her rejection by an avoidant
  lover. Jacques compassionately attempts to reunite the young woman with 
 her beau\, but his feelings for Marthe soon become less than platonic. Br
 esson’s previously hard-to-see version of Dostoevsky’s White Nights i
 s told in his inimitable minimalist style\, yielding surprisingly romanti
 c and emotional results. Plus\, Paris was never more beautifully photogra
 phed at night. A recent 4K restoration will be screened.\n\nhttps://cinem
 a.wisc.edu/events/four-nights-of-a-dreamer-2/\n\nSource: UW Cinematheque
URL:https://cinema.wisc.edu/events/four-nights-of-a-dreamer-2/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:cinematheque-2803-0-1791075600@cinema.wisc.edu
DTSTAMP:20261004T010000Z
DTSTART:20261004T010000Z
DTEND:20261004T025000Z
SUMMARY:TWO LOVERS
LOCATION:UW Cinematheque\, 4070 Vilas Hall\, 821 University Ave\, Madison\
 , WI 53706
DESCRIPTION:USA| 2008 | 35mm | 110 min.\n\nDirector: James Gray\n\nCast: J
 oaquin Phoenix\, Gwynneth Paltrow\, Vinessa Shaw\n\nPhoenix delivers a qu
 ietly devastating performance as Leonard\, a melancholic Brooklyn man tor
 n between the promise of a stable future with the kind-hearted Sandra (Sh
 aw) and his consuming obsession with his enigmatic neighbor Michelle (Pal
 trow). An American reimagining of Dostoevsky’s White Nights\, director 
 Gray (Paper Tiger\, We Own the Night) transforms the classic tale of unre
 quited love into a lived-in\, authentic portrait of contemporary New York
 ers yearning for human connection. “A film of unusual perception\, play
 ed at perfect pitch by Phoenix\, Shaw\, Paltrow and the other actors…It
  is calm and mature” (Roger Ebert).\n\nhttps://cinema.wisc.edu/events/t
 wo-lovers/\n\nSource: UW Cinematheque
URL:https://cinema.wisc.edu/events/two-lovers/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
