SUMMARY:DER FAN
LOCATION:UW Cinematheque\, 4070 Vilas Hall\, 821 University Ave\, Madison\
 , WI 53706
DESCRIPTION:West Germany | 1982 | DCP | 92 min.\n\nDirector: Eckhart Schmi
 dt\n\nCast: Désirée Nosbusch\, Bodo Steiger\, Simone Brahmann\n\nTeenag
 er Simone (Nosbusch) has developed a bordering-on-unhealthy obsession wit
 h pop singer R (Steiger). Abandoning school and her family to pursue her 
 fantasy\, Simone encounters her idol at a TV taping and the stage is set 
 for a coolly enacted nightmare of seduction\, rejection\, and revenge. Ee
 rily directed in a clinically detached style\, this hypnotic thriller was
  never given an official theatrical release in the U.S. until now. The 4K
  DCP restoration of the original German language version is provided by F
 un City Editions.\n\nhttps://cinema.wisc.edu/events/der-fan/\n\nSource: U
 W Cinematheque
URL:https://cinema.wisc.edu/events/der-fan/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:cinematheque-2809-0-1792350000@cinema.wisc.edu
DTSTAMP:20261018T190000Z
DTSTART:20261018T190000Z
DTEND:20261018T204000Z
SUMMARY:Zeitgeist Shorts
LOCATION:Chazen Museum of Art\, 750 University Ave\, Madison\, WI 53706
DESCRIPTION:16mm/35mm| 100 min.\n\nFar more than most distributors\, Zeitg
 eist Films goes to bat for visionary cinema that do not fit into the trad
 itional two-hour theatrical runtime. Their eclectic collection includes t
 he short film catalog of stop-motion masters Jan Švankmajer and The Brot
 hers Quay\, as well as the mind-bending early work of Peter Greenaway. Ze
 itgeist also celebrated new voices\, including the fascinating shorts pro
 duced by Apparatus\, a nonprofit founded by Todd Haynes and Christine Vac
 hon to support emerging filmmakers. The program includes Food (Švankmaje
 r\, 1992\, 17 min.)\, He Was Once (Mary Hestand\, 1989\, 16 min.)\, Stree
 t of Crocodiles (Quay\, 1986\, 21 min.)\, We’re Talking Vulva (Tracy Tr
 aeger & Shawna Dempsey\, 1990\, 5 min.)\, and A Walk Through H (Greenaway
 \, 1978\, 41 min.). (MK)\n\nhttps://cinema.wisc.edu/events/zeitgeist-shor
 ts/\n\nSource: UW Cinematheque
URL:https://cinema.wisc.edu/events/zeitgeist-shorts/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:cinematheque-2810-0-1792713600@cinema.wisc.edu
DTSTAMP:20261023T000000Z
DTSTART:20261023T000000Z
DTEND:20261023T014000Z
SUMMARY:ONCE UPON A TIME IN HARLEM
LOCATION:UW Cinematheque\, 4070 Vilas Hall\, 821 University Ave\, Madison\
 , WI 53706
DESCRIPTION:USA | 2026 | DCP| 100 mins.\n\nDirector: David Greaves\n\nOn a
  summer day in 1972\, many of the key figures of the Harlem Renaissance c
 onvened for a party at Duke Ellington’s apartment\, where they reminisc
 ed and debated their legacy. This lively\, once-in-a-lifetime reunion was
  captured on 16mm by Symbiopsychotaxiplasm director William Greaves. Over
  fifty years later\, that footage has finally been edited into this inval
 uable document\, a double reflection spanning 100 years of Black American
  art. “One of the greatest talking pictures\, one of the greatest cinem
 atic works of creative nonfiction\, that I’ve ever seen” (Richard Bro
 dy\, The New Yorker). (MK)\n\nhttps://cinema.wisc.edu/events/once-upon-a-
 time-in-harlem/\n\nSource: UW Cinematheque
URL:https://cinema.wisc.edu/events/once-upon-a-time-in-harlem/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:cinematheque-2526-0-1792800000@cinema.wisc.edu
DTSTAMP:20261024T000000Z
DTSTART:20261024T000000Z
DTEND:20261024T022300Z
SUMMARY:THE TASTE OF TEA
LOCATION:UW Cinematheque\, 4084 Vilas Hall\, 821 University Ave\, Madison\
 , WI 53706
DESCRIPTION:Japan | 2004 | DCP | 143 min. | Japanese with English subtitle
 s\n\nDirector: Katsuhito Ishii\n\nCast: Takahiro Sato\, Maya Sakano\, Tad
 anobu Asano\n\nIn the quiet Japanese countryside\, a series of surreal an
 d comic episodes explore a wildly unconventional family: an artist mother
  obsessed with animation\, a hypnotherapist father\, a lovestruck teenage
  son\, a daughter haunted by a double\, and an uncle troubled by ghosts o
 f the past. Director Katsuhito Ishii\, who contributed the anime sequence
  to Kill Bill\, crafts an eccentric portrait of domestic life that blends
  deadpan humor\, musical interludes\, animated flourishes\, and dreamlike
  imagery. Both playful and deeply odd\, The Taste of Tea is “the most c
 harming comedy in town\,” (Michael Phillips\, Chicago Tribune).\n\nhttp
 s://cinema.wisc.edu/events/the-taste-of-tea/\n\nSource: UW Cinematheque
URL:https://cinema.wisc.edu/events/the-taste-of-tea/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:cinematheque-2522-0-1792886400@cinema.wisc.edu
DTSTAMP:20261025T000000Z
DTSTART:20261025T000000Z
DTEND:20261025T015400Z
SUMMARY:AFTER DARK\, MY SWEET
LOCATION:UW Cinematheque\, 4082 Vilas Hall\, 821 University Ave\, Madison\
 , WI 53706
DESCRIPTION:USA | 1990 | 35mm | 114 mins.\n\nDirector: James Foley\n\nCast
 : Jason Patric\, Rachel Ward\, Bruce Dern\n\nIn this sun-baked neo-noir\,
  a former boxer/escapee from a mental hospital (Patric) hooks up with a l
 onely widow (Ward) and a ne’er-do-well ex-cop (Dern) to hatch a kidnapp
 ing plot that goes wrong from the start. Filled with a cavalcade of noir 
 archetypes\, After Dark\, My Sweet is\, along with Sam Peckinpah’s The 
 Getaway and Stephen Frears’s The Grifters\, among the best adaptations 
 of Jim Thompson novels. “One of the purest and most uncompromising of m
 odern film noir. It captures above all the lonely\, exhausted lives of it
 s characters” (Roger Ebert). (BR)\n\nhttps://cinema.wisc.edu/events/aft
 er-dark-my-sweet/\n\nSource: UW Cinematheque
URL:https://cinema.wisc.edu/events/after-dark-my-sweet/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:cinematheque-2812-0-1793318400@cinema.wisc.edu
DTSTAMP:20261030T000000Z
DTSTART:20261030T000000Z
DTEND:20261030T014000Z
SUMMARY:SOUL PATROL – J.M. Harper and Jesse Lewis in Person!
LOCATION:UW Cinematheque\, 4070 Vilas Hall\, 821 University Ave\, Madison\
 , WI 53706
DESCRIPTION:USA | 2026 | DCP| 100 mins.\n\nDirector: J.M. Harper\n\nAs a t
 eenager\, Ed Emanuel enlisted in the U.S. Army only to find himself in Vi
 etnam questioning what it meant to fight for freedom abroad while Black A
 mericans continued to struggle for equality at home. In Soul Patrol\, Ema
 nuel reunites the surviving members of Company F\, the Vietnam War’s fi
 rst all-Black Long Range Reconnaissance Patrol unit\, whose extraordinary
  story is brought to life through intimate conversations\, personal archi
 ves\, and rare Super 8 footage. This vital historical document is also a 
 moving exploration of brotherhood\, identity\, and the enduring search fo
 r healing decades after the war. Immediately following this screening wil
 l be an in-person discussion with journalist/war correspondent Jesse Lewi
 s and Soul Patrol filmmaker J.M. Harper\, winner of the U.S. Documentary 
 Competition Directing Award at this year’s Sundance Film Festival. A co
 -presentation of the Cinematheque\, African-American Studies at UW-Madiso
 n\, and the Wisconsin Veterans Museum.\n\nhttps://cinema.wisc.edu/events/
 soul-patrol/\n\nSource: UW Cinematheque
URL:https://cinema.wisc.edu/events/soul-patrol/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:cinematheque-2515-0-1793404800@cinema.wisc.edu
DTSTAMP:20261031T000000Z
DTSTART:20261031T000000Z
DTEND:20261031T013500Z
SUMMARY:THE HOLE
LOCATION:UW Cinematheque\, 4075 Vilas Hall\, 821 University Ave\, Madison\
 , WI 53706
DESCRIPTION:Taiwan\, France | 1998 | 35mm | 95 min. | Mandarin with Englis
 h subtitles\n\nDirector: Tsai Ming-Liang\n\nCast: Yang Kuei-Mei\, Lee Kan
 g-Sheng\, Miao Tien\n\nIn The Hole\, unusual interactions between two res
 idents (Lee and Yang) of an apartment building in an apocalyptic\, virus-
 ridden Taipei become the foundation for a one-of-a-kind movie musical. Go
 odbye\, Dragon Inn filmmaker Tsai Ming-Liang’s work is often harsh and 
 excruciatingly sad. Yet amid the static long takes and palpable lonelines
 s\, Tsai always finds clever ways to complicate the grimness of his subje
 cts\, tinkering with genre and bone-dry humor. One of Tsai’s finest fil
 ms\, The Hole will screen on a brand new 35mm print courtesy of Big World
  Pictures. (JM)\n\nhttps://cinema.wisc.edu/events/the-hole/\n\nSource: UW
  Cinematheque
URL:https://cinema.wisc.edu/events/the-hole/
END:VEVENT
